WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.261
A/B tests for AI features.

2
00:00:02.541 --> 00:00:05.080
A canary asks “is the new version broken?

3
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.771
” An A/B test asks “is it better?

4
00:00:08.051 --> 00:00:14.216
” Both compare randomized groups at the same time, and the same discipline applies with a few additions.

5
00:00:14.496 --> 00:00:16.992
Randomize by user, not by request.

6
00:00:17.272 --> 00:00:25.976
A user who gets a different assistant on every message has an incoherent experience, and their behavior in one arm contaminates the other.

7
00:00:26.256 --> 00:00:35.963
Choose one primary metric before you start, tied to the outcome the feature exists for: tickets resolved without a human, tasks completed, time to answer.

8
00:00:36.243 --> 00:00:49.363
Then list guardrail metrics that must not get worse even if the primary metric improves: refusal rate, guard false-positive rate, escalations, cost per conversation, p95 latency, complaint rate.

9
00:00:49.643 --> 00:00:57.771
A variant that resolves more tickets by confidently inventing policies will show up in the guardrails long before it shows up in revenue.

10
00:00:58.051 --> 00:00:59.160
Size the test.

11
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:07.461
Decide the smallest effect worth detecting and estimate the sample size you need for it, using the interval reasoning from Studio 3.

12
00:01:07.741 --> 00:01:12.840
Small differences on noisy metrics take far more traffic than intuition suggests.

13
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.296
Watch for novelty and learning effects.

14
00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:20.227
Users react to anything new at first, and they adapt to a model’s quirks over time.

15
00:01:20.507 --> 00:01:22.725
Run long enough to see past the first days.

16
00:01:23.005 --> 00:01:25.501
Use online evals alongside behavior.

17
00:01:25.781 --> 00:01:32.608
Users’ clicks tell you what they did; a calibrated grader on sampled traffic tells you whether the answers were right.

18
00:01:32.888 --> 00:01:36.685
You need both, because users often can’t tell when an answer is wrong.
